  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Prove that if $a$, $b$ and $c$ are all odd integers, then the equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ has no rational solution.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No rational solution exists.\n\nWriting a supposed root in lowest terms gives a parity contradiction in every case.",
      "worked": "Suppose a rational root exists. Write $x=\\frac{p}{q}$, where $p,q$ are integers, $q\\ne0$, and $p$ and $q$ have no common factor greater than $1$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}a\\left(\\frac pq\\right)^2+b\\left(\\frac pq\\right)+c&=0\\\\ap^2+bpq+cq^2&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nIf $p$ is even, then $q$ is odd. The first two terms are even and the third is odd, so their sum cannot be $0$.\n\nIf $q$ is even, then $p$ is odd. The first term is odd and the other two are even, again impossible.\n\nOtherwise $p$ and $q$ are both odd. All three terms are odd, so their sum is odd and cannot be $0$.\n\nEvery case gives a contradiction. Thus no rational solution exists.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 92
  },
